WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:12.245
[ ♫ Pamela Z’s Music ♫ ]

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:43.143
- When I first saw Pamela,
what I was really struck by was

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:48.481
this kind of holistic way in which
she approached technology, electronics.

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:51.518
And it was similar to the way
she approached her voice,

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:52.619
and in fact,

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:54.220
they bleed into each other

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.790
in really interesting ways,
and you can't have one without the other.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:21.381
[Pamela Z demonstrates gesture controls]

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:24.918
- I teach a digital instrument design class

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:27.687
where we learn to create
digital musical instruments.

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:30.190
So basically we're working with sensors
and microcontrollers,

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:31.658
and Pamela

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:33.426
has been doing exactly that.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.402
When she
came in and did her demonstration,

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:44.404
we talked about her sensors that she used,

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:47.340
we talked a lot about her
gestures, her approach to mapping.

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:51.010
- The instruments that I was using just now,
were that instrument,

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.178
which is my voice.

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:56.783
And then I was using several
different gesture control instruments

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.319
to manipulate the sounds,

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:01.321
sometimes just to play sampled sounds.

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:09.195
- Our students are very well educated

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:13.032
around the implications of systems
and how to implement things.

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:17.270
And to be able to get into a discussion
around the artistic

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.507
implications of those kinds
of decisions with an artist

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:24.110
who's living it all the time,
that's invaluable.

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.213
You can't get that kind of experience
really anywhere else.

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:28.882
- These instruments,

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:32.418
just like any other instruments,
have a gesture vocabulary that you learn,

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:34.420
and it comes from
playing the instrument

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.256
and how to get out of it
what you need.

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:38.791
When you're thinking
about instrument design,

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:42.295
does this instrument
give the user of the instrument

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:45.632
 room to be expressive
with the instrument?

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:50.603
- We're involved in technology
here every day,

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:53.706
but really like, what is it for?

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:56.676
And when you're talking about something
like music or the arts,

00:02:57.477 --> 00:03:01.481
I think it really is about helping people
express themselves

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:04.450
to make human experiences
that are interesting.

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:06.953
The focus always needs to be on,

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.088
how does this connect to people?

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:15.695
[ ♫ Pamela Z’s Music ♫ ]

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.235
- These tools,

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:25.471
machines, instruments,
whatever we're calling them,

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:29.342
I like to see those as something
that we express ourselves through them

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:33.746
rather than trying to train them
so that they

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:36.382
 can take over the expression.

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:46.893
Here, I feel like this whole idea of art

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:49.996
and technology is much more integrated

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:54.434
and seen as a continuum
and not as these like separate things.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:59.305
- I always feel like sometimes with like
electronic music that it can be so hard

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:03.209
to like, really feel like the audience
is always included in what's happening.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:07.013
- Pamela and I share conceptual ideas
about having that

00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:09.716
grounding in like our voices
being in the center of the material.

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:10.950
I think there's that connection

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.953
between how we feel about our voices,
how you feel about ourselves.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:17.624
It is the only instrument
that really has that sort of embodied

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:20.093
nature living with inside us.

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:23.596
And I think technology for me
is kind of situated in like emphasizing

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.632
that sense of embodied control.

00:04:25.665 --> 00:04:28.468
- I immediately started thinking,
what about larger?

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:30.103
And on a stand,

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:31.738
and so you're standing up

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:34.807
and now it's you're standing there,
you're with your audience,

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:38.077
and when you're manipulating it,
it's right in front of you.

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:39.946
And they see you,

00:04:39.946 --> 00:04:44.617
you know, manipulating it as opposed
to it's a small thing on your table

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:47.987
and it's down and you're here
and you're at the computer.

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:49.956
And, you know, it's
almost like the difference

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:54.060
between gesturing at someone
or pointing at someone versus

00:04:54.394 --> 00:04:57.497
putting your hand on their face
and stroking their cheek.

00:04:57.797 --> 00:05:02.568
- The assignment that I gave to
Evan's class was that they all go out

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:05.538
and record a found sound.

00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:06.973
[ ♫ Student Music ♫ ]

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:09.609
Sounds that were not necessarily intended

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:13.446
by the original maker
of that sound to be used in art.

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:18.351
And take that sound and make a very
short composition out of it.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.691
- It really expanded
what my definition of music was.

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:27.927
Before I thought it was just
instruments and vocal parts

00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:28.761
 and things like that.

00:05:28.761 --> 00:05:32.031
But now, like as I go about my daily life,
sometimes I'll hear a sound

00:05:32.031 --> 00:05:33.566
and I'll just like, record it.

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:37.970
- Wow, there was this great moment
where the voices got really dense.

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:41.574
Suddenly we maybe were
let into some place or something.

00:05:41.574 --> 00:05:43.176
It was a nice moment.

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:46.379
[ ♫ Pamela Z’s Music ♫ ]

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:50.917
- When somebody who's out there
in the world

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:53.820
doing the things that you admire

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:56.389
comes and spends time with you and pays

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:58.891
attention to you and tells you,

00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:01.327
“Oh, yeah,
what you're doing is interesting, too,

00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:03.463
and what you're doing
relates to what I'm doing.”

00:06:03.463 --> 00:06:06.332
Those are the moments
you remember as a student.

